http: headers written for one host do not follow a redirect to another

A player hands the connection the request headers its host wrote for a
media: a token for a media server, a name and a password for a WebDAV
share. A server may answer by sending the request on to another host (a
download node, a store of files), and FFmpeg then sent every one of
those headers there too.

With the `headers_elsewhere` option a caller states what to send in
place of `headers` once a redirect has led to a host of another name:
the same headers less the ones that say who is asking, which only the
caller can tell apart. They take the place of `headers` for the rest of
the connection's life, a seek and a reconnection included. Without the
option nothing changes. A redirect on the same host, to another port or
from HTTP to HTTPS, leaves the headers as they are.

The option is among those a playlist's connection hands on to the
requests the playlist leads to, so each of those is covered the same way.

